          "source_text_sha256": "b653e34c74211f442b797eed09e148d19385186edffd6cc34aeff0d171b65291",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "text": "Description: On April 6, 2024, around 9:14 PM, the witness was walking through the streets of Valenciennes when they observed two \"round shapes\" of orange color at a moderate altitude coming from the south. These objects moved silently and at high speed towards the witness. The latter reported the presence of a dark halo around the first object. Subsequently, the two PAN seemed to gradually disintegrate as they headed north, giving the impression that they were \"consuming\" themselves due to a lack of \"fuel.\" Only one testimony was collected on this matter. The consistency* of this case is considered satisfactory, given the quality of the testimony, the detailed information provided by the witness, and a video submitted as a supplement. *According to the criteria of GEIPAN, consistency is the amount of reliable and objective information gathered for a testimony. The hypothesis adopted (see the investigation report) is that of the observation of two celestial lanterns; it is based on the following elements:\n\n- Round shape and orange color, typical of lanterns\n- Compatibility of the wind with the apparent movement of PAN\n- Synchronized movement without noise\n- Possible observation of a halo, corresponding to the lantern's envelope\n- Observation duration of two minutes, compatible with the average lifespan of lanterns (approximately 6 to 8 minutes)\n- Observation of the extinction, as the lanterns ran out of fuel. This final phase is described perfectly by the witness.\n- The observation was made on a Friday evening: lanterns are most often released on weekends for celebrations.\n\nGEIPAN classifies the case as A: Celestial Lanterns.",
          "translation_text_sha256": "5e6fbc593f40867b4d658518b5c39958acf1afa10d1038dd4199ae7c9497e06a"
        }
